--- LCEL ---
Meridian Supply Co. delivered $8.46M in Q3 revenue across 5,579 orders with 33.1% gross margin and 102,433 units shipped, up 4.8% QoQ despite the loss of Hallow

Three objects and two pipes. That is genuinely less typing than the SDK, and the
pieces are swappable: change the model line and nothing else moves.

--- the same mistake, twice ---
A template variable is misspelled: {docment} instead of {document}.

LCEL       : KeyError
             "Input to ChatPromptTemplate is missing variables {'docment'}.  Expected: ['docment'] Rece
             7 frames, 6 of them inside site-packages
plain SDK  : KeyError
             'docment'
             1 frame, 0 of them inside site-packages

Be fair about which message is better: LangChain's names the variable it wanted
and the one it got, and the SDK's is the bare word 'docment'. On wording, the
framework wins.

The cost is the shape of the traceback. The SDK raises 1 frame deep, in
the line you typed. LCEL raises 7 frames deep, 6 of them in code you did not
write, and the failure is reported in the framework's vocabulary - a prompt
variable - rather than in yours.

That is the trade, and it is not an argument against frameworks. It is the
argument for hand-building first: you can read a stack trace through someone
else's abstraction only once you know what it is doing on your behalf.
